# Everything textual is LF, in the repository and in the working tree. AISH is a
# Linux-only program, so there is no platform that wants CRLF here. This rule
# exists because a Windows checkout once rewrote README.md to CRLF: nothing
# broke, but every later edit to that file produced a whole-file diff, which
# buries the actual change and makes `git diff --check` unusable. Without an
# explicit rule, git's default depends on each developer's core.autocrlf.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Terminal capture corpus: raw bytes recorded from a real PTY. These files ARE
# the escape sequences under test, so line-ending conversion would silently
# corrupt them — and with core.autocrlf enabled (any Windows checkout) that is
# the default behaviour for anything git classifies as text. The current
# captures happen to contain NUL bytes, so git's own heuristic detects them as
# binary, but that is luck rather than a guarantee: a regenerated capture
# without NULs would be mangled, and the resulting test failure would be
# baffling. Mark them explicitly instead. This rule must stay BELOW the
# wildcard above: for a path matched by several lines, the last one wins.
internal/term/testdata/*.bin binary
